{#- Who owns what on the board, which both modes of `follow-up-task.md.j2` carry whole. -#}
{% set re_estimate_command = "`" ~ re_estimate ~ " --board " ~ board ~ " --run " ~ run ~ " <the issue's id, as `<its board>:<id>`>`" %}
## Ownership on the board

- **Ownership is by run.** An issue on `{{ board }}`, or on a board it routes to, belongs to the run its `{{ shape.record_key }}`
  record's `created_by_run` names. A comment belongs to the run named in its **last line**,
  whichever of the two kinds below it is. This run, `{{ run }}`, may create, edit (by copying
  its ticket again, which a comment dispatch's settle step does for a ticket the dispatch
  changed) or close as not planned only issues whose `created_by_run` is `{{ run }}`,
  and may edit or delete only comments whose marker names `{{ run }}`. It never changes an
  issue or a comment belonging to another run, but for the one write below:
  {{ re_estimate_command }}.
- **{{ re_estimate_command }} re-estimates an issue's priority after this run comments on
  it.** It recounts the issue's occurrences off its comments, stores the estimate in its
  `{{ shape.record_key }}` record — bringing a schema-{{ shape.prior_schema }} record to schema {{ shape.schema }} — rewrites the
  `- {{ shape.estimate_line }}:` line of its `## {{ shape.impact }}` section, and moves its priority only while the
  board holds the estimate its record stored (or holds none, where it stored none), so a
  priority a person set stays. It changes nothing else about the issue, its status
  included. Run it on the issue each time this run adds or edits an evidence comment there,
  and each time it posts a reply marked `verdict="{{ shape.confirms }}"` there; never on any other
  occasion. A comment dispatch runs no re-estimate itself: its settle step makes the one each
  of its confirming replies is owed, counting from the snapshot's comments and the replies it
  verified rather than from a fresh read of the board's.
- **An evidence comment** carries this run's evidence on another run's issue. It belongs to
  the run its last line names, `{{ evidence_marker }}` for this run; one this run's `{{ comment }}`
  writes opens with one collapsed block summarised `{{ shape.comment_summary }}`, and one written
  before that block existed opens with the line `{{ evidence_opening }}`.

  **It carries only this run's new occurrence**, because the issue already states the
  ticket and a comment that re-renders it hides the one new fact it adds. Write it only with

  `{{ comment }} --board {{ board }} --run {{ run }} --root-cause <the issue's root_cause> --evidence-file <observed> --sources-file <sources> [--bears-on-file <bears on>] [--question-file <question>] <the issue's id>`

  from these parts, each a file you write, which it puts in their one order — the opening
  block, the parts, then the marker — so you never write an opening line, a block or a
  marker yourself:

  1. `--sources-file`: one JSON object holding exactly {{ shape.comment_source_keys }}, as a ticket's
     record holds them — the host read with `hostname`, the basis commit of each repository,
     the drafts this occurrence rests on (none only for a budget question) and one witness per
     verified claim, {{ shape.witness_shape }}. `{{ comment }}` reads each draft's source and transcript
     from the draft, and puts all of it in the comment's collapsed block;
  2. `--evidence-file`: what was observed, in prose, which the comment shows as written;
  3. `--bears-on-file`, optional: one short paragraph, which `{{ comment }}` writes after
     `{{ shape.bears_on }}`, naming the section of the issue the new evidence contradicts or
     extends (its root cause, impact or suggested fix) and stating only the delta, since this
     run cannot edit another run's ticket. It is **required where this run's evidence shows
     the root cause broader than the issue states**, and then states only the widened root
     cause, in the same plain words a ticket's `## Root cause` is written in, and names each
     location it adds as a contributing location of the suggested fix. A run widens its own
     issue by rewriting its ticket and copying it again instead;
  4. `--question-file`, for a budget question only: the question, which the comment shows as
     written.

  No part repeats what the issue already says — its root cause, impact, examples,
  suggested fix, rejected fixes or owning runs — and none carries a ticket's level-2
  section heading, current or retired ({{ shape.ticket_headings }}), or a `<details>` line.
  `{{ comment }}` refuses, before writing anything, a card outside `{{ board }}`'s family, an empty
  evidence file, a part carrying one of those headings or a `<details>` line, a root cause that
  is not a slug, and sources that do not validate.

  It goes only on an open issue another run created for the same root cause, which
  receives at most **one** from this run — whatever status the board holds it at, so an
  item at `Deferred` receives this run's one comment like any other open item. Where this
  run's evidence comment is already there, edit it in place with the same `{{ comment }}`
  command and `--edit <that comment's id>`, which refuses a comment whose marker does not name
  this run as its evidence comment; never add a second, and never edit it with a bare
  `{{ plan_store }} task comment edit`. This run never adds an evidence
  comment to an issue it created: it edits that issue by copying its ticket again instead.
- **A reply** answers one person's comment. Each comment the feedback below quotes under a
  `### Comment` heading, with its id and URL, gets exactly **one** new reply from this run,
  on the issue that holds that comment, whichever run owns that issue. Its last line is
  exactly

  `{{ reply_marker }}`

  where `<root-cause>` is the `root_cause` in the ticket record of the issue the reply is
  posted on, and `answers` is the id of the comment it answers, exactly as the feedback
  gives it. `verdict` is this run's judgment of the comment: `{{ shape.confirms }}` when it confirms
  the issue's root cause occurs — a new sighting of it, or evidence that it is more
  prevalent than the ticket says — and `{{ shape.does_not_confirm }}` for anything else: a question, a
  correction, a disagreement. A confirming reply counts as one more occurrence of the root
  cause. Its first line is visible to a reader: `{{ reply_opening }}`, or
  `{{ reply_opening_unattributed }}` when the feedback reports no author. After a blank line
  comes the response: what this run did about the comment and why, or why it did nothing;
  after another blank line, the marker. Post it with `{{ plan_store }} task comment add` — in a
  comment dispatch, with `--expect` and `--json` as its "Where everything is" says — after the
  actions it reports.
- **A reply is never edited to answer a different comment**, since every comment gets a
  reply of its own. A reply never counts as this run's one evidence comment, and never
  carries evidence in place of the ticket or the evidence comment.
- **Reply only to the comments the feedback quotes.** A comment that appears on the board
  during this dispatch is left for the next gathering, and feedback the manager wrote
  quotes no board comment, so it gets no reply.
